// Lesson 5.3 — Into the Cave: Scenes, Persistence & The Refactor
// The architectural payoff lesson, and the heaviest page in the course. The
// student walks into a cave (the switch has worked since they gave the Player
// a collider in 2.1), discovers that every number they own comes back reset,
// and refactors coins, health, maxHealth and the axe flag off PlayerController
// onto the StatManager singleton that has been an empty stub since Unit 2.
//
// Scaffolding: FULL WALKTHROUGH (level 1) — and TWO of them, because this
// lesson introduces two mechanisms with no precedent anywhere in the course.
// A SINGLETON (a static slot, an Awake that claims it, DontDestroyOnLoad, and
// a guard against the second copy) and an ON-SCREEN HUD (a Canvas, a TMP text
// object, and a script that reads the numbers from the manager instead of the
// player). The ladder is per-CONCEPT, not per-week, so both are level 1 and
// neither withholds a line. verify-site reads the level from EVERY walkthrough
// on the page, refuses a page whose walkthroughs disagree, and sums the gaps
// across all of them — so a second walkthrough that quietly withheld lines
// fails the build. The DECOMPOSE block is level 1 too: every part named,
// explained and sourced, because "keep my stats across a scene" has never been
// built here either.
//
// The old "Build Lab: The Refactor" spec callout is replaced by the singleton
// walkthrough (a level-1 lesson gives the code, not a list of questions about
// it), and the HUD spec callout by the HUD walkthrough. Two paragraphs from the
// old page survive deliberately: the trap explaining that a scene load
// REPLACES your stats rather than losing them, and the info callout explaining
// that this is a refactor and not a rewrite. The Canvas trap survives too, with
// the scene object named correctly (CaveSwitch) and the listener identified as
// an override on the scene INSTANCE rather than on the prefab.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read from the project, not assumed):
//   - Start (build index 0) holds the StatManager object, a Player, a Ground
//     Quad, a Cave Exit and Collision tilemaps — and NO Canvas. Overworld
//     (build 1) is the hub: Player, Coins, Enemies, Turrets, the CaveSwitch
//     lever and two cave entrances, plus a Canvas, a Text (TMP) and an
//     EventSystem. Platformer (build 2) has its own tilemaps and a Player,
//     plus an exit back to the overworld.
//   - The lever's onLeverOn event — on the CaveSwitch INSTANCE in the Overworld
//     scene, not on the prefab — drives THREE listeners: the two cave-door
//     tilemaps ("Collision Door Removed", "No Collision Door Removed") and the
//     scene's Canvas. So moving that Canvas to Start leaves the lever holding a
//     Missing reference.
//   - SceneSwitch.cs holds `[SerializeField] int scene` — an INT BUILD INDEX,
//     not a scene name. Overworld's two cave mouths hold scene = 0 (Start) and
//     scene = 2 (Platformer); the exits in Start and Platformer hold scene = 1
//     (Overworld). It also keeps `static int sceneSwitchedFrom` and `static
//     RuntimeAnimatorController playerAnimator`, restores the player's animator
//     controller on every arrival, and sets position/facing from the scene it
//     came from. It loads with SceneManager.LoadScene(scene). Reordering the
//     build's scene list silently repoints every serialized index — the bug
//     hunt's last item tests exactly that failure mode, and it is the one the
//     real shipped code has.
//   - StatManager.cs is still an EMPTY STUB (`public class StatManager :
//     MonoBehaviour { }`) until this lesson. PlayerController.cs is a stub in
//     the starter project; the student's own file has accumulated `coins`,
//     `health`, `maxHealth` (with the 2.3 cap) and the 5.1 axe bool over the
//     year, and 2.2's damage method is `TakeDamage(int amount)` with a positive
//     amount as the one that hurts.
//   - Unity 6: `Rigidbody2D.velocity` is `linearVelocity`, and
//     `Object.FindObjectOfType<T>()` is deprecated in favour of
//     `Object.FindFirstObjectByType<T>()`. TextMeshPro ships with
//     com.unity.ugui, so `TMPro` / `TextMeshProUGUI` is available.
