    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What it gives you</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>public static StatManager Instance;</code></td>
            <td>A field that belongs to the <strong>class</strong> rather than to an object — so any script can reach it without a reference, a search, or even knowing whether an object exists.</td>
            <td>There is exactly one, and it starts as <code>null</code>. Nothing claims it for you, and while it is <code>null</code> every caller that reads it throws.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>private void Awake()</code></td>
            <td>Unity's earliest hook: it runs once per object, and every <code>Awake()</code> in a scene finishes before the first <code>Start()</code> runs.</td>
            <td>It is early, not omniscient — you cannot assume another object's <code>Start()</code> has run, and other objects cannot assume yours has either. The order between two objects' <code>Awake()</code> methods is still not promised.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject)</code></td>
            <td>The one call that changes what a scene load is allowed to do. It moves the object into a scene of its own that never unloads, so it survives every load from then on.</td>
            <td>It takes a whole <strong>GameObject</strong>, not a component. And it survives everything — including a return to the scene it came from, where the scene's own copy will be waiting for it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Destroy(gameObject)</code></td>
            <td>Removes an object — how the newcomer copy takes itself out of the game.</td>
            <td>The removal is deferred to the end of the frame, not instant. It is gone in every practical sense, but a line that runs later in the same frame can still reach it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>SceneManager.LoadScene(int buildIndex)</code></td>
            <td>What <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> calls every time you touch a cave mouth — the line that is destroying your stats, in the shipped code of your own project.</td>
            <td>Hand it an <code>int</code> and it loads whatever scene sits at that <em>position</em> in the build list. Reorder the list and every serialized index silently points somewhere else, with no error to warn you.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Object.FindFirstObjectByType&lt;T&gt;()</code></td>
            <td>How you would find the manager on demand, with no instance field at all — and the current name for it, because Unity 6 <strong>deprecated</strong> the older <code>FindObjectOfType</code> that most tutorials still use.</td>
            <td>A scene-wide search every time you call it. That search is exactly the cost the <code>static</code> field removes, which is why nothing in this lesson searches for anything.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>TextMeshProUGUI.text</code></td>
            <td>The property that holds the characters a UI label is displaying. Assign a string to it and the screen changes.</td>
            <td>Every assignment builds a new string, so doing it every frame costs something; and the component lives in the <code>TMPro</code> namespace, so the file needs <code>using TMPro;</code> or the type cannot be found.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>You have already seen persistence in this project — done the wrong way.</strong>
